event: response.output_text.done
data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_09b20607c3feb39e006a7bae7b97d48190b5e24c7d8951a1fc","logprobs":[],"output_index":2,"sequence_number":415,"text":"Here are the most recent notable updates about NASA’s **Perseverance** rover (from the latest reporting surfaced via Bing):\n\n- **Perseverance imaged Earth disappearing behind Mars’ moon Phobos (an “occultation”)**: NASA highlighted a Mastcam‑Z sequence from **July 2, 2026** showing Earth as a small bright point in the Martian sky moving and then vanishing behind Phobos. NASA published this update on **Aug. 5, 2026**. 【5:0†source】\n\n- **Perseverance hit a major driving milestone: a full “marathon” on Mars**: The rover has now driven **26.2 miles (42.195 km)** total, reaching that distance on **Sol 1890** (about five years and four months after landing), and doing it much faster than Opportunity did. This update was reported in mid‑July 2026 and references a new orbital image showing the rover’s tracks. 【5:1†source】\n\n- **First AI-planned driving routes executed on Mars**: NASA/JPL reported that Perseverance successfully completed drives whose routes (waypoints) were planned by **generative vision-language AI**, demonstrated on **Dec. 8 and 10, 2025** and announced **Jan. 30, 2026**. The effort was led by JPL with collaboration involving Anthropic’s Claude models. 【5:3†source】\n\n- **Science finding: complex organic carbon detected in Jezero Crater rocks**: A June 2026 report describes researchers using Perseverance data to identify **complex organic carbon** in mudstones (Bright Angel outcrop / Neretva Vallis area). It’s not presented as proof of past life, but it strengthens evidence that organics can persist on Mars over geologic time. 【5:4†source】\n\nIf you tell me whether you want **only NASA/JPL official updates** or also **science-journal/news coverage**, I can filter the “latest news” accordingly."}

